<div class="uplift-case"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">Uplift Academy Ohio · 2026 impact stories</p><h1>From first lines of code<br/>to ideas for the world.</h1><p class="meta">MidPointe Library & Mason Public Library, Ohio</p></header><p class="intro">Uplift began with a simple idea: give younger students a place to learn, people to learn with, and the chance to create something of their own. Our inaugural NextGen Coders cohort became the foundation for a summer of innovation.</p>
<nav class="case-nav" aria-label="Uplift programs"><a href="#cohort">01 · Inaugural cohort</a><a href="#summer-camp">02 · Summer innovation camp</a></nav>
<section class="section" id="cohort"><p class="eyebrow">01 · Where it began</p><h2>NextGen Coders.<br/>A first step worth taking.</h2><p class="meta">January 6–March 17, 2026 · 10-week bootcamp · Mason Public Library, Ohio</p><p class="intro">Thirty elementary school students joined our inaugural pilot for twice-weekly sessions in coding, creative problem solving, and digital discovery. Participation was free.</p>
<div class="case-stats">{stats.cohort.map(s=><div><strong>{s.value}</strong><span>{s.label}</span></div>)}</div><p class="source">Program-reported results for the January–March 2026 pilot. These replace the earlier preliminary cohort figures and are separate from summer camp results.</p>
<Photo id="cohort-02" hero alt="Two volunteer instructors introducing student presentation prompts at the library" caption="Making space for students to explain what they built—and how they built it."/>
<div class="two-col"><div><p class="eyebrow">The starting point</p><h3>A library, a volunteer team,<br/>and room to experiment.</h3></div><div><p>We wanted children to experience technology as creators. For families facing the cost of enrichment programs, a free program in a familiar community space offered a way in.</p><p>Using Code.org alongside hands-on activities, our volunteer instructors introduced digital citizenship, internet safety, algorithms, and sequencing. Puzzles and collaborative challenges made loops, debugging, and logical reasoning tangible.</p><p>The aim was to give students enough structure to begin—and enough freedom to ask questions, make mistakes, and work out why a solution worked.</p></div></div>
<ol class="camp-process"><li><span class="stage-count">01</span><h3>Find the foundations</h3><p>Digital citizenship, sequencing, and algorithms.</p></li><li><span class="stage-count">02</span><h3>Try, test, debug</h3><p>Puzzles, loops, and hands-on challenges.</p></li><li><span class="stage-count">03</span><h3>Create with code</h3><p>Artwork, animations, stories, and games.</p></li><li><span class="stage-count">04</span><h3>Think together</h3><p>Collaboration, persistence, and problem solving.</p></li><li><span class="stage-count">05</span><h3>Share the work</h3><p>Original projects and a community showcase.</p></li></ol>
</section>
<div class="case-photo-pair"><Photo id="cohort-03" alt="A student demonstrating a space-themed digital project with a mentor beside the lectern" caption="A student takes the lead in explaining a digital creation."/><Photo id="cohort-04" alt="A student and mentor discussing an animated Scratch project on the projection screen" caption="From working on a screen to sharing an idea with a room."/></div>
<section class="section two-col"><div><p class="eyebrow">Students teaching students</p><h2>The learning went<br/>both ways.</h2><Photo id="cohort-01" alt="A young learner receiving a completion certificate beside the volunteer team" caption="Celebrating the effort behind a completed first cohort."/></div><div><p>High school volunteers contributed more than 450 service hours to lesson preparation, instruction, individual mentorship, and coordination. Younger students had someone to turn to when a project stalled; volunteers took responsibility for helping someone else learn.</p><p>Across the pilot, 30 learners received more than 600 student learning hours. The final showcase welcomed over 60 students, family members, volunteers, and supporters to celebrate their work.</p><p>For me, the significance of this first cohort was the model it put into practice: a public library, student mentors, a structured curriculum, and opportunities for children to make and present their own projects.</p><p>That foundation carried into NextGen Innovation Camp, where coding became a tool for exploring wider challenges.</p></div></section>
<section class="section" id="summer-camp"><p class="eyebrow">02 · Building on the foundation</p><h2>NextGen Innovation Camp.</h2><p class="meta">July 18–August 8, 2026 · MidPointe Library & Mason Public Library, Ohio</p><p class="intro">The next chapter connected computer science with design thinking and global challenges, giving students a public stage for the ideas they made their own.</p></section><Photo id="09" hero alt="Students holding up their illustrated team posters in a library program room" caption="The people behind the numbers. Students share their team posters during the camp."/>
<div class="case-stats">{stats.camp.map(s=><div><strong>{s.value}</strong><span>{s.label}</span></div>)}</div><p class="source">Program-reported results for summer 2026. Student learning hours and volunteer service hours measure different contributions and are not combined.</p>
<nav class="case-nav" aria-label="Case study sections"><a href="#access">The challenge</a><a href="#approach">The approach</a><a href="#showcase">The showcase</a><a href="#service">The volunteers</a><a href="#cohort">The inaugural cohort</a></nav>
<section class="section two-col" id="access"><div><p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p><h2>Curiosity is everywhere.<br/>Access isn’t.</h2></div><div><p>I wanted younger students to explore technology without cost deciding who could participate. Through Uplift Academy Ohio, we made NextGen Innovation Camp completely free for elementary and middle school students.</p><p>The goal went beyond learning to code. We wanted students to notice a problem, ask who it affected, and discover that they could begin working on an answer.</p><p>My role involved program development, volunteer coordination, and the planning that helped bring students, families, and community spaces together. The ideas themselves belonged to the students.</p></div></section>
<div class="case-photo-pair"><Photo id="10" alt="Students working on laptops while volunteers support a Scratch lesson" caption="A room full of questions, projects, and people ready to help."/><Photo id="04" alt="Students and volunteers gathered around a table for a paper bridge challenge" caption="The paper bridge challenge brought hands-on engineering into the learning experience."/></div>
<section class="section" id="approach"><p class="eyebrow">From learning to leading</p><h2>A path from “how does this work?”<br/>to “here’s what I made.”</h2><p class="intro">Computer science, design thinking, and the United Nations’ 17 Sustainable Development Goals gave students a starting point. Mentorship gave them room to keep going.</p><ol class="camp-process">{stages.map(([name,title,text],i)=><li><span class="stage-count">0{i+1}</span><p class="eyebrow">{name}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p></li>)}</ol></section>
<div class="case-photo-pair"><Photo id="06" alt="A laptop displaying a lesson about Sustainable Development Goals in front of a group of students" caption="Global challenges became a starting point for students’ own questions."/><Photo id="07" alt="Students working together on large presentation boards in a library room" caption="Research and ideas begin to take shape on presentation boards."/></div>
<section class="section" id="showcase"><div class="two-col"><div><p class="eyebrow">The innovation showcase</p><h2>70+ ideas.<br/>17 global goals.<br/>A room ready to listen.</h2></div><div><p>The camp ended with an Innovation Showcase attended by more than 200 family members, mentors, and community members. Students presented more than 70 original innovation ideas, collectively representing all 17 Sustainable Development Goals.</p><p>What stayed with me was watching students explain the work they had spent time developing. The planning and volunteer coordination mattered because they helped make that moment possible: someone had an idea, and other people were listening.</p><p>The projects were proposals and prototypes. Their value here was in the process of researching a challenge, developing a response, testing it, and explaining it—not a claim that students had already solved those global problems.</p></div></div></section>
<div class="case-project-gallery"><Photo id="01" alt="A Life on Land project display about SDG 15 with a digital game on a laptop" caption="Life on Land: research and a digital prototype presented together."/><Photo id="02" alt="A student beside a Life Below Water project display and laptop game" caption="Life Below Water: a student presents a response to an environmental challenge."/><Photo id="03" alt="A laptop showing economic growth research beside a colorful innovation display" caption="Economic growth: connecting research, design, and a proposed solution."/></div>
<section class="section two-col" id="service"><div><p class="eyebrow">Powered by service</p><h2>850+ hours of showing up.</h2><Photo id="05" alt="A volunteer leading a small-group discussion around a library table"/></div><div><p>Volunteers supported instruction, mentored students, helped troubleshoot projects, and kept the experience moving. Their collective contribution exceeded 850 service hours during the camp.</p><p>For me, this was an important part of the story: young people giving their time so that younger students could try something new. The program depended on that day-to-day work as much as on its curriculum.</p><p>Hosting the camp at MidPointe Library and Mason Public Library brought the experience into public community spaces. Across the summer, students received more than 3,500 learning hours.</p><p>Those hours describe the scale of the opportunity. The photos show the work inside it: questions, collaboration, patient explanations, and repeated attempts.</p></div></section>
<div class="case-photo-pair"><Photo id="08" alt="Students at laptops and volunteers moving between tables during a library workshop" caption="Learning in a shared space, with help close by."/><Photo id="11" alt="A presenter addressing students and families seated in a bright library room" caption="Bringing students and families into the conversation."/></div>

<section class="section two-col"><div><p class="eyebrow">What I’m taking forward</p><h2>Make the opportunity real.<br/>Then make room.</h2></div><div><p>This camp reinforced why I wanted to build Uplift: a student’s financial circumstances should not decide whether they can explore an interest.</p><p>I want to keep creating experiences where young people can test an idea, get help when they need it, and share something they are proud of. The next step is to carry what we learned about teaching, mentorship, and community partnerships into future programs.</p><a href="https://upliftohio.org/">Explore Uplift Academy Ohio ↗</a></div></section>
<details><summary>More about Uplift Academy Ohio</summary><p>President & founder, Ohio division · founded March 2025. My work includes program development, volunteer coordination, and community partnerships.</p><p>Beyond NextGen Coders and Innovation Camp, my source account describes NextGen Mentors, supporting younger STEM learners; and Uplift Sparks, designed for children receiving motor therapy. Current Uplift Sparks delivery status and planned expansion into Title I schools remain to be confirmed.</p><p><a href={path('journal/innovation-showcase/')}>Read my journal reflection →</a></p></details><aside class="note"><strong>About this record.</strong> Program results, host locations, and photographs were supplied by Varshith in November 2026. Figures are program-reported, not independently audited. Learning hours, volunteer hours, attendees, and ideas are distinct measures; no combined “lives impacted” total is claimed. The inaugural cohort ran January 6–March 17, 2026; the summer camp ran July 18–August 8, 2026. The specific showcase date and methodology behind hour totals have not been supplied. <a href={path('evidence/#figures')}>Source notes →</a></aside></div>
